{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the beginning.</b> At the beginning of time — the first, indivisible instant, before which there was no time at all." "<b>Created.</b> He brought into being what was not; with this, no time at all elapses." "<b>Elohim.</b> The word אֱלוֹהַּ denotes the eternal; therefore concerning the demons, who die like mortals, as our Sages of blessed memory testified, it says: לַשֵּׁדִים לֹא אֱלוֹהַּ (Deuteronomy 32:17). And it says of the blessed El, אֱלוֹהַּ, for He is the eternal in the absolute sense, as it says: וַיִּטֹּשׁ אֱלוֹהַּ עָשָׂהוּ. And the plural form אֱלֹהִים is said of Him to indicate that He is the form of all eternal forms and whatever is besides them, as it says: מְלֹא כָל הָאָרֶץ כְבוֹדוֹ (Isaiah 6:3) — for nothing besides Him has existence independent of that emanated from His existence. And no existent besides His existence is found, as it says: וְאַתָּה מְחַיֶּה אֶת כֻּלָּם (Nehemiah 9:6). And by analogy, every being separate from matter is called אֱלֹהִים, and expert judges are called אֱלֹהִים inasmuch as they judge in the image of Elohim. And to indicate the supremacy of His eternity, from which the eternity of all other separate beings is emanated, it is stated that He is Elohei ha-Elohim (Elohim of the Elohim)." "<b>Et the heavens.</b> The word שָׁם denotes a far-off place, and every plural sign with an initial patach from above indicates two equals. Thus the word שָׁמַיִם denotes a body far-off in relation to us, in two equal distances from every side. This cannot occur except in a sphere encompassing at the extremity of a circle. He says, then, that He created that body which is now far from us from every side in two equal distances — and that is the sphere. Therefore he did not say בָּרָא שָׁמַיִם, for this cannot be said of it in itself but only in its relation to our location." "And he said <b>and et the earth</b> — the center proper to the sphere."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Reading זְמַן כְּלָל as 'any time whatsoever' — i.e., creation ex nihilo involves no temporal duration.", 2 "Sforno's citation וַיִּטֹּשׁ אֱלוֹהַּ עָשָׂהוּ appears to be Deuteronomy 32:17 (זָבְחוּ לַשֵּׁדִים לֹא אֱלוֹהַּ); he may be quoting from memory with a variant phrasing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:10:59.527876001Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}